  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers/few storms this evening should diminish overnight\nwith fog developing. Drying conditions returning for the early \nhalf of the coming week.\n\nShortwave per mesoanalysis and WV loop is over the Cumberland\nPlateau of TN and should shift east over our area overnight but\nweakening. Should see best concentration of showers/few storms\nthis evening, but again not everyone will have rain.\n\nOnce the wave moves east, fog may form with some clearing\nespecially east of the mountains.\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\nApproaching upper shortwave energy should allow the boundary\nthat was near the VA/NC border this morning to lift northward as\na warm front along with increased deep layer moisture. Some\nconvective initiation has already occurred with some scattered\nshowers pushing northward in the CWA. Coverage should increase\nlater this afternoon and evening as the upper forcing kicks in\nmore. Morning stratus lingered on for a while so destabilization\ncould be limited - progged MLCAPE is around 1000 J/kg and the  \nmorning sounding indicated some tall skinny CAPE profile. Shear\nparameters are weak so all in all any thunderstorms should be\nyour generic garden variety but possible training could lead to\nsome localized flooding. A few strong storms could be possible\nfrom downburst winds. Decent coverage could last through the\nevening and there is some CAM consensus on this. Trough axis\nshould make it's way into the area by Sunday morning and allow\nfor a mostly dry second half of the weekend. \n\nA weak cold front should push southward across the area for\nMonday though this should be mainly a dry passage and with a\nridge building in behind this feature at least the first half \nof the work week looks to remain dry as well.",
